{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D38","rows":38,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 2 :  Ocean Hydrodynamics & Ship Motions",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alaçam  |  Naval Architect & Marine Engineer  |  Türkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"What is Volume 2?","This volume builds on Volume 1 (Ship Design Fundamentals) by introducing the dynamic behaviour of ships and offshore structures in a real ocean environment. While Volume 1 asked 'does this ship float and is it stable?', Volume 2 asks 'what happens when waves arrive?' — the forces, motions, and engineering responses.",null],[null,"Why does this matter?","Real ships operate in waves. A bulk carrier crossing the North Atlantic, an FPSO on a deepwater field, a monopile wind turbine foundation — all must be designed to withstand wave forces throughout their working lives. Ocean hydrodynamics is the physics that connects the sea state to the structural loads.",null],[null,"Prerequisites","Volume 1 of this toolkit, or equivalent knowledge of basic naval architecture: displacement, stability, resistance. Undergraduate-level mathematics: trigonometry, basic calculus concepts (integrals appear in wave energy and spectral analysis — they are explained, not assumed).",null],[null,"How to use","Start with WAVE PARAMETERS — enter wave height and period. All other sheets update automatically. Gold cells with blue text are your inputs. Read the explanation boxes — they tell you WHY each formula exists.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","This page — introduction and guide"],[null,"2","WAVE PARAMETERS","Master input — Airy wave theory, wavelength, speed, energy"],[null,"3","WAVE STATISTICS","Irregular seas, H1/3, Rayleigh distribution, Beaufort scale"],[null,"4","WAVE SPECTRA","PM and JONSWAP spectra, spectral moments, sea state description"],[null,"5","SHIP MOTIONS (6 DOF)","Natural periods of heave, pitch, roll — resonance conditions"],[null,"6","ADDED RESISTANCE","Power increase in waves, speed loss, sea margin, Kwon method"],[null,"7","MORISON EQUATION","Wave forces on cylinders — offshore jacket legs, monopiles"],[null,"8","OFFSHORE STRUCTURES","Fixed vs floating platforms, air gap, mooring tension basics"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Wave tables, Beaufort scale, sea states, Cm/Cd values"],[null,"10","CHARTS & GRAPHS","Wave profile, PM/JONSWAP spectra, roll RAO, Morison force"],[null,null,null,null],[null,"COLOUR LEGEND",null,null],[null,"Blue text on gold",null,"USER INPUT — change these cells"],[null,"Black text on white",null,"FORMULA — do not edit"],[null,"Grey text on blue",null,"EXPLANATION — read these"],[null,"Green highlighted",null,"KEY RESULT"],[null,"Amber highlighted",null,"WARNING / CHECK THIS"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alaçam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. No part of this document may be reproduced without prior written permission of the author. Knowledge must be free! , share with students and educators.",null,null],[null,"All formulas are derived from published sources cited in each sheet. Empirical approximations are clearly identified. This workbook is for educational understanding — professional ship design requires full numerical analysis and Classification Society review.",null,null]]}
