{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D32","rows":32,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 3 :  Propeller, Machinery & Marine Materials",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alaçam  |  Naval Architect & Marine Engineer  |  Türkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"⚠  EDUCATIONAL DISCLAIMER  ⚠",null,null],[null,"All formulas are derived from published sources cited in each sheet. Empirical approximations are clearly identified. This workbook is for educational understanding — professional ship design requires full numerical analysis and Classification Society review. The Wageningen B-series polynomial coefficients used here are the published values from Oosterveld & van Oossanen (1975) — the standard reference.",null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The Trilogy","This is Volume 3 of the Naval Architecture Teaching Toolkit. Vol.1 answered: does the ship float and is it stable? Vol.2 answered: what happens when waves arrive? Vol.3 answers: how does the ship move through the water, what turns the propeller, and what materials make it all possible?",null],[null,"Why propellers?","The propeller is the most complex component on a ship. It operates in the turbulent wake behind the hull, at high Reynolds numbers, subject to cavitation, corrosion, and fatigue. Understanding propeller design links fluid mechanics, materials science, and mechanical engineering in one beautiful object.",null],[null,"Why metallurgy?","Ships are made of materials — and the ocean is one of the most corrosive environments on earth. Every material choice is a battle between strength, weight, cost, and corrosion resistance. A naval architect who does not understand galvanic corrosion will design structures that destroy themselves.",null],[null,"Disclaimer","All formulas sourced and cited. Empirical approximations are identified. Professional propeller design requires full CFD, model testing, and class approval. This workbook teaches the principles, not the final design tool.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","This page — introduction, disclaimer, guide"],[null,"2","PROPELLER GEOMETRY","Diameter, pitch, slip, advance coefficient J, KT, KQ"],[null,"3","WAGENINGEN B-SERIES","Published polynomial KT/KQ, open water efficiency η_0"],[null,"4","CAVITATION (BURRILL)","Thrust loading, Burrill diagram, cavitation check"],[null,"5","ENGINE MATCHING","Propeller curve, MCR/NCR, fuel consumption, range"],[null,"6","SHAFT DESIGN","Shaft diameter, shear stress, whirling speed, bearing"],[null,"7","PROPELLER FLUID DYN.","Blade lift/drag, wake fraction, hull & propulsive efficiency"],[null,"8","MARINE METALLURGY","Material properties, galvanic series, cathodic protection"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","B-series coefficients, material data, SFOC, galvanic series"],[null,"10","CHARTS & GRAPHS","KT/KQ/η_0 open water diagram, propeller-engine matching"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alaçam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. No part of this document may be reproduced without prior written permission of the author. Knowledge must be free!  share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
