{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D32","rows":32,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 4 :  Structural Design & Scantlings",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alaçam  |  Naval Architect & Marine Engineer  |  Türkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"⚠  EDUCATIONAL DISCLAIMER  ⚠",null,null],[null,"All scantling formulas in this workbook are sourced from published Classification Society rules and structural engineering textbooks — every formula is cited. This workbook teaches structural PRINCIPLES and calculation METHODS. Professional ship structural design requires full Classification Society rule compliance, FEA analysis, and approved drawings. Never use this workbook as the sole basis for a real structure. All formulas are reproduced for educational purposes under fair use.",null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The Quadrilogy","Vol.1: Does the ship float? Vol.2: What do waves do to it? Vol.3: How does it move? Vol.4: Does the steel hold? This volume closes the loop — structural design is where physics, materials science, and risk management meet. A structural failure at sea is usually catastrophic and irreversible.",null],[null,"Why scantlings?","'Scantling' is the naval architectural term for the dimensions of a structural member — the thickness of a plate, the size of a stiffener. Scantlings are determined by loads (hydrostatic pressure, wave bending, cargo weight) and material resistance (yield strength, buckling, fatigue). Too thin = failure. Too thick = excess weight = less cargo = less profit.",null],[null,"What you will learn","Hull girder bending (hogging and sagging), plate thickness from first principles, stiffener design, buckling — the silent killer, fatigue over 10⁸ cycles, transverse structural strength, hatch covers and deck loading. Every formula explained, every number sourced.",null],[null,"Disclaimer","Formulas from LR, DNV, BV, IACS rules — reproduced for education only. Real structural design needs full rule compliance and class approval. Corrosion additions, stress concentration factors, and fatigue details are simplified here — the real rules have hundreds of additional clauses.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, disclaimer, worksheet guide"],[null,"2","HULL GIRDER BENDING","SWBM, WBM, section modulus, hogging/sagging"],[null,"3","PLATE SCANTLINGS","Bottom/side/deck plate thickness — LR/DNV formulas"],[null,"4","STIFFENERS & FRAMES","Section modulus, moment of inertia, neutral axis"],[null,"5","BUCKLING","Euler column, Bryan plate buckling, interaction formula"],[null,"6","FATIGUE","S-N curves, Palmgren-Miner, hot spot stress, fatigue life"],[null,"7","TRANSVERSE STRENGTH","Double bottom, bulkhead plating, tank pressure, racking"],[null,"8","SPECIAL STRUCTURES","Hatch covers, deck loading, crane pedestal, bilge keel"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Steel sections, yield strengths, corrosion additions, Kt"],[null,"10","CHARTS & GRAPHS","BM/SF diagrams, S-N fatigue curve, buckling interaction"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alaçam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. No part of this document may be reproduced without prior written permission of the author. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
