{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 5 :  Ship Manoeuvring & Control",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alaçam  |  Naval Architect & Marine Engineer  |  Türkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"What is manoeuvring?","Ship manoeuvring is the study of how a ship responds to helm and thrust commands in confined waters, open sea, and harbour approaches. A ship that cannot turn tightly enough, stop quickly enough, or hold course reliably is a hazard to itself, its crew, and the marine environment. The IMO Resolution MSC.137(76) sets mandatory minimum manoeuvring standards for all ships over 100m — every ship built today must meet these criteria.",null],[null,"Why does this matter?","The majority of serious maritime accidents occur during manoeuvring in confined waters: port approaches, canal transits, berthing operations. The Costa Concordia (2012), Exxon Valdez (1989), and numerous collision incidents all involved manoeuvring failures. Understanding the physics of turning, stopping, and course-keeping saves lives and protects the environment.",null],[null,"The series so far","Vol.1: The ship floats. Vol.2: Waves arrive. Vol.3: Propulsion. Vol.4: The steel holds. Vol.5: The ship turns, stops, and steers. Together the five volumes form a complete introduction to naval architecture from first principles.",null],[null,"Disclaimer","All formulas are sourced from published textbooks and IMO resolutions. Manoeuvring prediction methods shown here are empirical approximations. Actual ship manoeuvring characteristics must be established by model tests or full-scale trials as required by SOLAS and IMO.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, series overview, disclaimer"],[null,"2","MANOEUVRING BASICS","Master inputs — ship, speed, rudder geometry, non-dim coefficients"],[null,"3","TURNING CIRCLE","Advance, transfer, tactical diameter, heel in turn, IMO check"],[null,"4","STOPPING & CRASH STOP","Track reach, head reach, stopping distance, IMO check"],[null,"5","RUDDER DESIGN","Rudder area, lift, drag, torque, stock diameter, types"],[null,"6","BOW THRUSTER","Thrust force, tunnel diameter, power, effectiveness vs speed"],[null,"7","COURSE KEEPING","Directional stability, weather helm, wind force, yaw checking"],[null,"8","IMO CRITERIA","Full MSC.137(76) criteria table with calculated pass/fail"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Manoeuvring data by vessel type, rudder ratios, wind coefficients"],[null,"10","CHARTS & GRAPHS","Turning circle trajectory, rudder lift/drag, thruster effectiveness"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alaçam Naval Architecture. All rights reserved. No part of this document may be reproduced without prior written permission of the author. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
