{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D32","rows":32,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 6 :  Submarine Engineering",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alaçam  |  Naval Architect & Marine Engineer  |  Türkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"CRITICAL DISCLAIMER — PLEASE READ BEFORE USING THIS WORKBOOK",null,null],[null,"ALL content in this workbook is derived EXCLUSIVELY from OPEN, DECLASSIFIED, and PUBLICLY AVAILABLE literature including: R. Burcher & L. Rydill 'Concepts in Submarine Design' (Cambridge University Press, 1994), D.J. Eyres & G.J. Bruce 'Ship Stability for Masters and Mates', US Navy NAVSEA declassified design data, and published academic papers. NO classified or restricted information is included. This workbook is for EDUCATIONAL PURPOSES ONLY. Actual submarine design requires classified specifications, national security clearances, and decades of specialist experience. The formulas here teach PRINCIPLES — they are not design tools for real submarines. The pressure hull collapse formulas are from textbooks freely available in any university library worldwide.",null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why submarines?","Submarines represent the most extreme application of naval architecture. They must float on the surface, sink precisely to any depth, maintain neutral buoyancy while submerged, resist crushing external pressure, and do all of this silently and invisibly. Every principle from the previous five volumes appears here in its most demanding form. Understanding submarine engineering deepens understanding of everything else.",null],[null,"What is covered","Pressure hull design under external pressure (Von Mises, ring frame buckling), buoyancy and diving systems (main ballast tanks, trim tanks), surfaced and submerged stability (fundamentally different physics), resistance and propulsion submerged, hotel load and endurance, ring frame structural design, and an introduction to acoustic signatures. All from open literature — Burcher & Rydill (1994) is the primary reference.",null],[null,"Key difference from surface ships","Surface ships float because buoyancy exceeds weight. A submarine must achieve EXACT neutral buoyancy when submerged — weight must equal displaced weight precisely. Too heavy: it sinks uncontrolled. Too light: it rises uncontrolled. The hydrostatic pressure at depth tries to CRUSH the hull — this is the dominant structural load, the opposite of a surface ship where wave bending is the dominant load.",null],[null,"Sources","Burcher R. & Rydill L. (1994) Concepts in Submarine Design. Cambridge University Press. Comstock J.P. (ed.) (1967) Principles of Naval Architecture. SNAME. NAVSEA (1967) Design Data Sheet DDS 079-1 (declassified). Pulos J.G. & Salerno V.L. (1961) Axisymmetric elastic deformation of thin shells. DTMB.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, disclaimer, primary references"],[null,"2","SUBMARINE GEOMETRY","Hull form, L/D ratio, displacement surfaced/submerged"],[null,"3","PRESSURE HULL","Von Mises hoop stress, collapse depth, safety factors"],[null,"4","BUOYANCY & DIVING","MBT volume, reserve buoyancy, neutral buoyancy, trim tanks"],[null,"5","STABILITY","Surfaced GM, submerged BG stability, righting moment"],[null,"6","RESISTANCE & PROPULSION","Submerged drag, hotel load, battery endurance, AIP"],[null,"7","RING FRAME DESIGN","Frame section modulus, inter-frame collapse, general instability"],[null,"8","ACOUSTIC SIGNATURES","Noise budget, quieting measures, magnetic signature, degaussing"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Material properties, safety factors, submarine data, pressure table"],[null,"10","CHARTS & GRAPHS","Pressure vs depth, collapse vs frame spacing, speed-power, BG diagram"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alaçam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
