{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 7 :  Fluid Mechanics for Naval Architects and Marine Engineers",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why fluid mechanics?","Fluid mechanics is the physics underneath everything in naval architecture. Hydrostatics tells you whether a ship floats. Bernoulli explains how a propeller blade generates thrust. Reynolds number determines whether the boundary layer is laminar or turbulent, which governs friction resistance. Froude number governs wave-making resistance and explains why model tests work. You cannot truly understand ships without understanding the fluid they live in.",null],[null,"What is different about this volume","Generic fluid mechanics textbooks teach Bernoulli, Reynolds, and the Navier-Stokes equations in the abstract. This volume teaches every concept through a specific ship or ocean application. Bernoulli appears through a ship speed sensor and a ballast pump. Reynolds number appears through hull friction and cavitation. Froude number appears through model testing and wave resistance. Every formula has a ship behind it.",null],[null,"How to use this volume","This volume stands alone as an introduction to fluid mechanics for engineers entering the maritime field. It also serves as the theoretical foundation for Volumes 1 through 6. A student who works through all seven volumes will understand not just how to apply the formulas, but why they are the way they are. All inputs are on gold cells. All formulas are cited. Nothing is made up.",null],[null,"Primary references","White F.M. (2016) Fluid Mechanics 8th ed. McGraw-Hill. Munson B.R. et al. (2013) Fundamentals of Fluid Mechanics 7th ed. Wiley. Massey B. (2012) Mechanics of Fluids 9th ed. Spon Press. Rawson K.J. and Tupper E.C. (2001) Basic Ship Theory Vol.1 5th ed. ITTC (1999) Recommended Procedures.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, philosophy, primary references"],[null,"2","FLUID PROPERTIES","Density, viscosity, vapour pressure, surface tension, seawater"],[null,"3","HYDROSTATICS","Pressure distribution, forces on surfaces, buoyancy from first principles"],[null,"4","CONTINUITY and BERNOULLI","Conservation of mass, Bernoulli, Venturi, Pitot tube, pipe flow"],[null,"5","VISCOUS FLOW","Boundary layers, Reynolds number, skin friction, Moody diagram, hull fouling"],[null,"6","FLOW AROUND BODIES","Lift, drag, potential flow, separation, streamlining, rudder and blade sections"],[null,"7","DIMENSIONAL ANALYSIS","Buckingham Pi, Froude, Reynolds, Weber, Strouhal, model testing, ITTC"],[null,"8","PUMPS and PIPE SYSTEMS","Pump curves, system curves, NPSH, cavitation, series and parallel, ballast"],[null,"9","WAVE MAKING","Kelvin wave pattern, wave interference, resistance humps and hollows, sloshing"],[null,"10","REFERENCE TABLES","Fluid properties, Moody diagram data, drag coefficients, pump affinity laws"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
