{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[["x",null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 8 :  Damage Stability",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why damage stability?","Intact stability (Volume 1) tells us whether a ship is safe in its designed loading condition. Damage stability tells us whether it survives after an accident. Every major maritime disaster in history has a damage stability dimension. The Titanic (1912), Herald of Free Enterprise (1987), Estonia (1994), and Costa Concordia (2012) all involved flooding that exceeded the ship's ability to survive. Understanding damage stability is not academic. It is a matter of life and death at sea.",null],[null,"Two fundamental methods","The LOST BUOYANCY METHOD treats flooded compartments as if they no longer exist -- their buoyancy is simply removed. The ship sinks until the remaining intact waterplane supports it. The ADDED WEIGHT METHOD treats the flood water as extra cargo -- it adds weight and lowers the ship. Both give the same final waterline but differ in how they handle free surface effects and intermediate stages. This volume teaches both, with full calculations.",null],[null,"SOLAS requirements","The International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS) mandates damage stability standards for all passenger ships and most cargo ships. SOLAS 2009 Chapter II-1 introduced the probabilistic method -- instead of checking specific damage cases, a statistical index A is calculated and must exceed a required index R. This volume covers both the deterministic and probabilistic approaches.",null],[null,"References","Barras C.B. (2004) Ship Stability for Masters and Mates 6th ed. Rawson K.J. and Tupper E.C. (2001) Basic Ship Theory Vol.1 5th ed. SOLAS Consolidated Edition (2020) Chapter II-1. IMO MSC.1/Circ.1246 (2008) Explanatory Notes on SOLAS Chapter II-1. Eyres D.J. (2007) Ship Stability for Masters and Mates 6th ed.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, methods overview, SOLAS context"],[null,"2","DAMAGE STABILITY BASICS","Key terminology, permeability, margin line, subdivision"],[null,"3","LOST BUOYANCY METHOD","New waterline, new draft and trim, new GM after flooding"],[null,"4","ADDED WEIGHT METHOD","Flood water as added weight, free surface, comparison"],[null,"5","FLOODABLE LENGTH","Floodable length calculation, factor of subdivision, permissible length"],[null,"6","SOLAS PROBABILISTIC","Attained index A, required index R, si and pi factors"],[null,"7","RESIDUAL STABILITY","Post-damage GZ, IMO residual criteria, heel after flooding"],[null,"8","PROGRESSIVE FLOODING","Cross flooding, downflooding angles, intermediate stages"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","SOLAS requirements, permeability values, index R formulas, IMO criteria"],[null,"10","CHARTS AND GRAPHS","Floodable length curve, residual GZ vs intact GZ, flooding probability"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
