{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 9 :  Introduction to Finite Element Methods for Naval Architects",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"What this volume teaches","This volume teaches the mathematics that ANSYS, Abaqus, Nastran, and every other FEA software package uses underneath their graphical interfaces. Not how to click buttons in a software. The actual linear algebra. K times u equals F. Stiffness matrix times displacement vector equals force vector. When you understand this equation, FEA becomes transparent rather than a black box. Sheet 10 contains a complete working FEA solver built entirely in Excel using MINVERSE and MMULT. Real results. Real matrix algebra. No software required.",null],[null,"Why naval architects need FEA","IACS Common Structural Rules (CSR-BC and OT 2023) mandate direct structural calculation by FEA for bulk carriers and tankers over 150m. Lloyd's Register, DNV, and Bureau Veritas all require FEA for hatch corner analysis, web frame fatigue, cargo hold strength, and collision assessment. A naval architect who cannot read and critically assess an FEA report cannot approve a ship structural drawing. This volume gives the conceptual foundation to do exactly that.",null],[null,"What FEA cannot do","FEA gives wrong answers when the mesh is too coarse. FEA gives wrong answers when boundary conditions are wrong. FEA gives wrong answers when the material model is inappropriate. A student who treats FEA output as truth without understanding the assumptions is more dangerous than a student who has no FEA at all. This volume teaches not just how FEA works but where and why it can fail.",null],[null,"References","Cook R.D. et al. (2002) Concepts and Applications of Finite Element Analysis 4th ed. Wiley. Hughes T.J.R. (2000) The Finite Element Method. Dover Publications. IACS Common Structural Rules for Bulk Carriers and Oil Tankers (CSR-BC and OT 2023). Zienkiewicz O.C. and Taylor R.L. (2000) The Finite Element Method Vol.1 5th ed. Butterworth.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, philosophy, references"],[null,"2","WHAT IS FEA","Concept, discretisation, nodes, elements, DOFs, where mandatory"],[null,"3","STIFFNESS MATRIX","K x u = F explained, spring elements, matrix assembly in Excel"],[null,"4","BAR ELEMENT","Axial stiffness matrix, assembly, BCs, displacements and stresses"],[null,"5","BEAM ELEMENT","Euler-Bernoulli beam, 4x4 stiffness matrix, two-span beam solved"],[null,"6","ASSEMBLY AND BCs","Direct stiffness method, boundary conditions, load vectors, frame"],[null,"7","STRESS AND FAILURE","Stresses from displacements, principal stresses, Von Mises criterion"],[null,"8","FEA IN SHIP DESIGN","CSR requirements, mesh types, hot spot stress, fatigue link"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Element types, DOFs, software comparison, CSR mesh requirements"],[null,"10","LIVE FEA SOLVER","Complete working plane frame FEA solver in Excel -- MINVERSE and MMULT"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
