{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 10 :  Hovercraft Engineering",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"A vehicle unlike any other","The hovercraft (Air Cushion Vehicle, ACV) sits at the intersection of naval architecture, aerodynamics, and mechanical engineering. It flies on a cushion of pressurised air trapped beneath its hull. It can cross water, land, mud, ice, and beach in the same journey. No other vehicle type offers this amphibious capability. Christopher Cockerell demonstrated the principle in 1955 using two tin cans and a vacuum cleaner. The SR.N1 crossed the English Channel in 1959. By 1968 the SR.N4 was carrying 254 passengers and 30 cars across the Channel in 35 minutes at 65 knots.",null],[null,"The fundamental physics","One equation drives almost everything in hovercraft design: Cushion pressure = Weight / Cushion area. p_c = W / A_c. This single relationship connects the lift fan power, the skirt geometry, the structural weight, and the payload capacity. Everything else in this volume flows from it. The cushion pressure is typically only 100 to 500 Pa -- less than 0.5% of atmospheric pressure -- yet it supports the entire vehicle.",null],[null,"Modern applications","Military assault craft: the US Navy LCAC carries a 60-tonne payload at 40 knots and is the primary means of ship-to-shore assault. Arctic logistics: hovercrafts are the only year-round surface vehicle that can cross Arctic sea ice reliably. Passenger ferries: Griffon and AP1-88 class craft still operate worldwide. Flood rescue: hovercrafts can reach survivors in flooded areas impassable to boats. Survey and inspection: small autonomous hovercrafts for tidal flat survey.",null],[null,"References","Yun L. and Bliault A. (2000) Theory and Design of Air Cushion Craft. Butterworth-Heinemann. ISBN 0-340-67650-7. Doctors L.J. (1997) The Hydro-Aerodynamics of Air-Cushion Vehicles. SNAME Transactions. Mantle P.J. (1980) Air Cushion Craft Development. DTNSRDC Report 80-012. IMO MSC/Circ.1166 (2005) Guidelines for the Design and Construction of Motor Vessels.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, physics overview, references"],[null,"2","HOVERCRAFT BASICS","Types, terminology, design parameters, Cockerell principle"],[null,"3","CUSHION PRESSURE","p_c = W/A_c, hover gap, skirt geometry, cushion power"],[null,"4","AERODYNAMIC RESISTANCE","Profile drag, momentum drag, wave making, total resistance"],[null,"5","PROPULSION","Air propeller thrust, fan efficiency, power split, engine matching"],[null,"6","SKIRT DESIGN","Bag and finger geometry, skirt pressure, drag, material, wear"],[null,"7","STABILITY","Cushion metacentre, roll and pitch stability, heave damping"],[null,"8","STRUCTURAL DESIGN","Weight budget, aluminium structure, cushion loads, structural index"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Known craft data, air properties, skirt materials, fan performance"],[null,"10","CHARTS AND GRAPHS","Cushion pressure vs hover height, resistance vs speed, power curve"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free! Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
