{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 11 :  Classification Societies and Marine Regulations",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The missing piece","The previous ten volumes taught you how to design a ship. This volume teaches you how to get it approved, certified, and kept in service. Every calculation in Volumes 1 through 10 ultimately exists to satisfy a rule, a convention, or a standard set by the institutions described here. Without this volume the toolkit is incomplete. A naval architect who cannot navigate the regulatory framework is like a structural engineer who can calculate stresses but does not know building codes exist.",null],[null,"Why this matters in practice","On the first day in a shipyard or design office, a junior naval architect encounters class rules, flag state requirements, SOLAS certificates, MARPOL compliance, and ISM audits. These are not taught in most universities. Students learn them slowly and expensively on the job. This volume gives that knowledge on day one, for free. It explains what Lloyd's Register is and why it was founded in a coffee house. It explains why a ship needs a IOPP certificate. It explains what happens when a PSC inspector detains your ship.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","The classification society system and its history. The 13 IACS member societies. The plan approval and survey process. SOLAS in plain language. MARPOL Annex I through VI. The ISM Code and MLC 2006. Port State Control and what a detention means. EEDI, EEXI, and CII -- the new carbon intensity regulations that are reshaping ship design right now. All from open public IMO and IACS documents.",null],[null,"References","IMO (2020) SOLAS Consolidated Edition. IMO (2017) MARPOL Consolidated Edition. IACS (2023) Unified Requirements -- published at iacs.org.uk. IMO (2018) ISM Code 2018 Edition. Paris MOU (annual) Annual Report on Port State Control -- public domain. Lloyd's Register (2023) Rules and Regulations for the Classification of Ships.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, purpose, references"],[null,"2","WHAT IS CLASS","History, purpose, the Lloyd's Coffee House, how class works"],[null,"3","CLASS SOCIETIES","All 13 IACS members, non-IACS societies, specialisations"],[null,"4","PLAN APPROVAL","Submission process, what gets checked, milestones, timeline"],[null,"5","SURVEYS","Annual, intermediate, special survey, COC, suspension, detention"],[null,"6","SOLAS","History, all key chapters in plain language, certificates"],[null,"7","MARPOL","Annexes I to VI, IOPP, EEDI, EEXI, CII regulations"],[null,"8","ISM AND MLC","ISM Code, DOC, SMC, MLC 2006, crew rights, rest hours"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Societies, certificates, survey intervals, PSC statistics"],[null,"10","CHARTS AND GRAPHS","Fleet by class, PSC detentions, CII targets, certificate timeline"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free! -Share with students and educators.",null,null]]}
