{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D29","rows":29,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 12 :  Advanced Resistance and Powering -- Holtrop-Mennen 1984",null,null],[null,"An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The Holtrop-Mennen method","The Holtrop-Mennen method is the most widely used statistical resistance prediction method in the world. It was developed at MARIN (Maritime Research Institute Netherlands) by Jan Holtrop and G.G.J. Mennen through regression analysis of model test results for 334 ship models covering a wide range of ship types and sizes. The 1984 paper by Holtrop alone extended and corrected the original 1982 paper, particularly for ships with bulbous bows and transom sterns. This volume implements the 1984 paper exclusively. Every coefficient, every conditional formula, and every equation number is traced to that single source.",null],[null,"Why this volume exists","Volume 1 of this toolkit used the Admiralty Coefficient: a single-equation approximation that gives a rough power estimate from displacement and speed. It is useful for a first guess but reveals nothing about hull form. The Holtrop-Mennen method breaks resistance into eight physically meaningful components: frictional, form, appendage, wave, bulb, transom, correlation, and air. Each component responds to specific hull form parameters. A designer can see exactly which component dominates and what to change. This is how professional resistance prediction is done at concept design stage.",null],[null,"What this volume does NOT do","This volume does not replace model testing or CFD for final design. Holtrop-Mennen is accurate to approximately 5 to 10 percent for ships within its validity range. Outside that range (very high Froude numbers, unusual hull forms, sailing yachts, planing craft) it can be significantly in error. The method is explicitly valid for: Fn from 0 to 0.45, Cp from 0.55 to 0.85, L/B from 3.9 to 14.9, B/T from 2.1 to 4.0. Validity checks are included on every input sheet.",null],[null,"Primary reference","Holtrop J. (1984) A statistical re-analysis of resistance and propulsion data. International Shipbuilding Progress 31(363):272-276. This is the ONLY source for all regression coefficients in this volume. Secondary reference for context only: Holtrop J. and Mennen G.G.J. (1982) An approximate power prediction method. International Shipbuilding Progress 29(335):166-170.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, validity range, primary reference"],[null,"2","METHOD OVERVIEW","MARIN database, validity limits, method comparison, workflow"],[null,"3","HULL FORM INPUTS","All hull parameters, derived coefficients, validity checks"],[null,"4","FRICTIONAL RESISTANCE","RF from ITTC-1957, form factor (1+k1), appendage resistance"],[null,"5","WAVE RESISTANCE","Full Holtrop 1984 RW formula, all c-coefficients, m1 m2 lambda"],[null,"6","ADDITIONAL RESISTANCES","Bulb correction RB, transom RTR, correlation RA, air resistance"],[null,"7","PROPULSIVE COEFFICIENTS","Wake w, thrust deduction t, relative rotative efficiency etaR"],[null,"8","DELIVERED POWER","Shaft power PD, brake power PB, engine matching, speed-power curve"],[null,"9","EEDI AND CII","EEDI calculation, required index by phase, CII annual rating"],[null,"10","CHARTS AND GRAPHS","Speed-power curve, resistance breakdown, EEDI compliance"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free!",null,null]]}
