{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D29","rows":29,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 13 :  Seakeeping and Operability",null,null],[null,"The Final Volume -- An Open Educational Resource for Engineering Students",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The missing dimension","Every volume in this toolkit from Vol.1 to Vol.12 assumed calm water. Hydrostatics: calm water. Resistance: calm water. Propulsion: calm water. Structural loads: wave bending moments derived from statistics, not from motion. But ships spend their entire working lives in waves. Seakeeping is the science of how a ship actually behaves at sea. It answers the questions that matter most to the people on board: Will the ship capsize in this storm? Will the cargo shift? Will the crew be incapacitated by seasickness? Can the helicopter land safely? This volume provides the theoretical and practical tools to answer those questions.",null],[null,"What seakeeping covers","MOTIONS: heave, pitch, roll, surge, sway, yaw -- the six degrees of freedom and their natural periods, damping, and resonance conditions. WAVE ENVIRONMENT: how the sea is described statistically through spectra. RESPONSE AMPLITUDE OPERATORS: the transfer function between wave input and ship response. OPERABILITY: the systematic assessment of whether a ship can perform its mission in a given sea state, based on quantified limits for motion, acceleration, slamming, deck wetness, and crew incapacitation. SPEED LOSS: how much speed a ship voluntarily or involuntarily loses in bad weather.",null],[null,"Why this is the right final volume","Seakeeping connects everything else in the encyclopedia. It uses the hull form coefficients from Vol.1. It uses the wave spectra from Vol.2. It uses the stability parameters from Vol.1 and Vol.8. It uses the structural loads from Vol.4. It uses the propulsion power from Vol.12. A naval architect who understands all thirteen volumes understands a ship as a complete system in its real operating environment.",null],[null,"Primary references","Lloyd A.R.J.M. (1989) Seakeeping: Ship Behaviour in Rough Weather. Ellis Horwood. Faltinsen O.M. (1990) Sea Loads on Ships and Offshore Structures. Cambridge University Press. IMO Resolution A.749(18) (1993) Code on Intact Stability Chapter 2.3 Weather Criterion. Kwon Y.J. (2008) Speed loss due to added resistance in wind and waves. The Naval Architect. RINA. NORDFORSK (1987) Assessment of Ship Performance in a Seaway. Nordic Cooperative Project. Olson S.R. (1978) An Analysis of the Seakeeping Characteristics of Fast Monohulls. DTNSRDC Report 78-0083.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, encyclopedia completion, references"],[null,"2","SEAKEEPING BASICS","Six DOF, natural periods, resonance, damping, terminology"],[null,"3","WAVE ENVIRONMENT","PM and JONSWAP spectra, sea states, significant wave height"],[null,"4","RAO THEORY","Response amplitude operators, strip theory concept, RAO shapes"],[null,"5","ROLL MOTION","Roll period, damping, bilge keels, fin stabilisers, IMO criterion"],[null,"6","HEAVE AND PITCH","Heave and pitch periods, added mass, slamming, added resistance"],[null,"7","OPERABILITY","MSI, vertical acceleration, deck wetness, slamming, operability limits"],[null,"8","SPEED LOSS","Kwon (2008) method, Beaufort scale, voluntary and involuntary loss"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Criteria table, sea states, Beaufort, natural periods, MSI limits"],[null,"10","CHARTS AND GRAPHS","Roll RAO, PM spectrum with ship period, speed loss vs Beaufort"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. Knowledge must be free!",null,null]]}
