{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 14 :  Offshore Structures and Drilling Platforms",null,null],[null,"Fixed Platforms, Floating Systems and Drilling Rigs",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The offshore engineering challenge","Offshore structures face a fundamentally different design problem from ships. A ship moves through waves. An offshore structure must resist waves while staying in position, producing oil and gas, and keeping people alive, sometimes in water depths exceeding 3,000 metres, for 25 to 30 years without dry docking. The loads are enormous. A 100-year storm wave in the North Sea can be 30 metres high. The structural response must be calculated for millions of wave cycles over the platform lifetime. The mooring system must hold position in hurricane conditions. This volume introduces the engineering tools to address these challenges.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Fixed structures: jacket platforms, gravity base structures, jack-up rigs. Floating structures: semi-submersibles, FPSOs, tension leg platforms, SPARs. Mooring systems: catenary mechanics, chain tension, offset criteria. Drilling systems: drill string loads, riser tension, hook load, BOP stack weight. Environmental loads: Morison equation wave forces on cylinders, wind, current. All from published open literature. Zero hallucinations. Every formula cited.",null],[null,"Connection to previous volumes","This volume builds directly on the teaching toolkit foundations. Wave theory from Vol.2. Structural design and fatigue from Vol.4. Damage stability of floating platforms from Vol.8. Seakeeping of semi-submersibles and FPSOs from Vol.13. Resistance and powering of drillships from Vol.12. The offshore engineer needs all of these simultaneously.",null],[null,"Primary references","Chakrabarti S.K. (1987) Hydrodynamics of Offshore Structures. Computational Mechanics. API RP 2A-WSD (2014) Recommended Practice for Fixed Offshore Platforms. API. DNV-OS-C101 (2021) Design of Offshore Steel Structures. DNV. Morison J.R. et al. (1950) The force exerted by surface waves on piles. Trans AIME 189:149-157. SNAME TR-5-5A (2002) Guidelines for Site Specific Assessment of Mobile Jack-Up Units. API RP 2SK (2005) Design and Analysis of Stationkeeping Systems. API. DNV-RP-C203 (2016) Fatigue Design of Offshore Steel Structures. DNV. Barltrop N.D.P. and Adams A.J. (1991) Dynamics of Fixed Marine Structures. Butterworth-Heinemann.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, references, worksheet index"],[null,"2","STRUCTURE TYPES","All offshore structure types, water depth ranges, history"],[null,"3","WAVE LOADS","Airy wave theory, Morison equation, total force and moment"],[null,"4","JACKET PLATFORM","Tubular members, joint design, pile capacity, unity check"],[null,"5","JACK-UP RIG","Preload, spudcan, leg strength, overturning, airgap"],[null,"6","SEMI-SUBMERSIBLE","Waterplane, metacentric height, heave period, stability"],[null,"7","FPSO DESIGN","Hull form, turret mooring, catenary tension, storage"],[null,"8","MOORING SYSTEMS","Catenary mechanics, line tension, offset, fatigue"],[null,"9","DRILLING SYSTEMS","Hook load, drill string, riser tension, BOP weight"],[null,"10","REFERENCE TABLES","Wave heights by region, Cm Cd coefficients, chain grades"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved. ",null,null]]}
