{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 15 :  Marine Corrosion and Corrosion Protection",null,null],[null,"Electrochemistry, Cathodic Protection and Coating Systems",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why corrosion matters","Seawater is one of the most aggressive corrosive environments on earth. It contains dissolved oxygen, chloride ions, sulphates, and biological organisms. A steel ship hull without protection loses approximately 0.1 to 0.3 mm per year. Over a 25-year service life that is 2.5 to 7.5 mm of lost steel, enough to compromise structural integrity entirely. The global cost of marine corrosion exceeds 50 billion USD per year. Every naval architect, marine engineer, and offshore engineer must understand why corrosion happens and how to stop it.",null],[null,"Connection to earlier volumes","Vol.4 used corrosion addition values t_c in structural scantlings. Vol.6 discussed pressure hull material selection for submarines. Vol.14 noted marine growth effects on Morison drag coefficient Cd. This volume provides the electrochemistry and protection design theory behind all of those references. It completes the structural and materials coverage of the toolkit.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Electrochemistry fundamentals: the corrosion cell, Nernst equation, Faraday's Law, corrosion rate from current density. Galvanic corrosion: galvanic series in seawater, area effects, material pairing rules. Eight corrosion types including pitting, crevice, MIC and SCC. Corrosion rates for ship structures from IACS data. Impressed current cathodic protection ICCP design. Sacrificial anode design using DNV-RP-B401 formulas. Coating systems from ISO 12944 and IMO PSPC. Offshore corrosion zones: splash zone to buried zone.",null],[null,"Primary references","Shreir L.L., Jarman R.A. and Burstein G.T. (1994) Corrosion. 3rd ed. Butterworth-Heinemann. NACE SP0176 (2007) Corrosion Control of Steel Fixed Offshore Platforms. NACE International. DNV-RP-B401 (2021) Cathodic Protection Design. DNV. ISO 12944 (2018) Paints and Varnishes -- Corrosion Protection of Steel Structures. IACS UR S31 (2012) Allowable Thickness Diminution for Hull Structure. Evans U.R. (1960) The Corrosion and Oxidation of Metals. Edward Arnold. Faraday M. (1834) Philosophical Transactions Royal Society 124:77-122. Melchers R.E. (2003) ASCE J. Engineering Mechanics 129(11):1121-1229. Dwight H.B. (1936) Trans AIEE 55:1319-1328.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, connection to earlier volumes, references"],[null,"2","ELECTROCHEMISTRY","Corrosion cell, Nernst equation, Faraday's Law, corrosion rate"],[null,"3","GALVANIC CORROSION","Galvanic series, area effects, dissimilar metal pairing rules"],[null,"4","CORROSION TYPES","Eight forms: uniform, pitting, crevice, erosion, MIC, SCC"],[null,"5","CORROSION RATES","Ship corrosion rates by location, thickness diminution, Melchers model"],[null,"6","ICCP DESIGN","Impressed current cathodic protection: current, anodes, rectifier"],[null,"7","SACRIFICIAL ANODES","Zn, Al, Mg anodes: Dwight resistance, mass calculation, DNV-RP-B401"],[null,"8","COATING SYSTEMS","ISO 12944, IMO PSPC, DFT, antifouling, coverage calculation"],[null,"9","OFFSHORE ZONES","Five corrosion zones, splash zone allowance, CP demand by zone"],[null,"10","REFERENCE TABLES","Galvanic series 25 metals, corrosion rates, anode properties, charts"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
{"kind":"table","sheet":"ELECTROCHEMISTRY","address":"A1:E32","rows":32,"cols":5,"values":[[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"ELECTROCHEMISTRY FUNDAMENTALS -- WHY CORROSION HAPPENS",null,null,null],[null,"Corrosion is an electrochemical process. It requires four components: an ANOD...",null,null,null],[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"THE CORROSION CELL -- FUNDAMENTAL REACTIONS",null,null,null]],"valuesTruncated":true,"valuesPreviewRows":5,"valuesPreviewCols":5,"valuesPreviewAddress":"A1:E5"}
