{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D30","rows":30,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 16 :  Marine Vibration and Noise",null,null],[null,"From Single Degree of Freedom Theory to Ship Hull and Machinery Vibration",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why vibration matters","Vibration is the enemy of structural integrity, crew comfort, and equipment reliability at sea. A ship is simultaneously a flexible beam vibrating in waves, a pressure vessel excited by its own machinery, and an acoustic radiator. The propeller creates pressure pulses at blade passing frequency. The main engine creates excitation at every harmonic of its firing frequency. Waves create impulsive slamming loads that excite the hull girder. Get any of these wrong and the consequences range from crew fatigue through to structural fatigue cracking and catastrophic failure.",null],[null,"Connection to earlier volumes","Vol.3: propeller design governs blade passing frequency and tip clearance. Vol.4: hull girder second moment of area I_m is used in the Todd formula for hull natural frequency. Vol.9: modal analysis is the vibration application of FEM stiffness matrices. Vol.13: slamming creates whipping excitation of the hull girder. This volume connects all of those threads into a unified vibration framework.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","SDOF theory from first principles: natural frequency, damping, resonance, magnification factor, transmissibility. Two-DOF and modal analysis: mode shapes, tuned mass damper. Hull girder vibration: Todd formula, added hydrodynamic mass, springing, whipping. Propeller-induced vibration: blade passing frequency, tip clearance rule, hull pressure fluctuations. Machinery vibration: engine excitation orders, torsional critical speeds. Vibration isolation: mount design, static deflection, isolation efficiency. Noise criteria: ISO 6954, IMO MSC/Circ.1006, dB calculations.",null],[null,"Primary references","Thomson W.T. and Dahleh M.D. (1998) Theory of Vibration with Applications 5th ed. Prentice Hall. Blevins R.D. (1979) Formulas for Natural Frequency and Mode Shape. Van Nostrand Reinhold. ISO 6954 (2000) Mechanical Vibration -- Guidelines for Ships. ISO. DNVGL-CG-0060 (2016) Vibration of Ship Hull and Equipment. DNV. Carlton J.S. (2018) Marine Propellers and Propulsion 4th ed. Butterworth-Heinemann. Ch.21. Timoshenko S. and Young D.H. (1955) Vibration Problems in Engineering 3rd ed. Van Nostrand. Todd F.H. (1961) Ship Hull Vibration. Edward Arnold. ISO 10816-1 (1995) Mechanical Vibration -- Evaluation of Machine Vibration. ISO. IMO MSC/Circ.1006 (2000) Code on Noise Levels on Board Ships.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, connections to earlier volumes, references"],[null,"2","SDOF THEORY","Free and forced vibration, damping, magnification factor, transmissibility"],[null,"3","TWO-DOF AND MODAL","Two-mass system, mode shapes, tuned mass damper"],[null,"4","HULL VIBRATION","Todd formula, added mass, 2-node frequency, springing, whipping"],[null,"5","PROPELLER VIBRATION","Blade passing frequency, tip clearance, hull pressure fluctuation"],[null,"6","MACHINERY VIBRATION","Engine excitation orders, torsional stiffness, critical speeds"],[null,"7","VIBRATION ISOLATION","Mount design, static deflection, transmissibility, isolation efficiency"],[null,"8","NOISE AND CRITERIA","ISO 6954, IMO noise limits, dB calculations, VDV"],[null,"9","REFERENCE TABLES","Natural frequency formulas, damping ratios, severity scale, BPF table"],[null,"10","CHARTS AND GRAPHS","MF vs r, TR vs r, BPF vs shaft speed"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
{"kind":"table","sheet":"SDOF THEORY","address":"A1:E81","rows":81,"cols":5,"values":[[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"SINGLE DEGREE OF FREEDOM VIBRATION -- FREE AND FORCED RESPONSE",null,null,null],[null,"The single degree of freedom (SDOF) oscillator is the foundation of all vibra...",null,null,null],[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"SDOF SYSTEM INPUTS",null,null,null]],"valuesTruncated":true,"valuesPreviewRows":5,"valuesPreviewCols":5,"valuesPreviewAddress":"A1:E5"}
