{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 19 :  Marine Diesel Engines",null,null],[null,"Two-Stroke and Four-Stroke Propulsion Engines, Turbocharging, Fuels and Emissions",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The most important prime mover in history","The marine diesel engine carries 90% of world trade. Every container of goods, every tonne of crude oil, every bag of grain that crosses an ocean does so powered by a slow-speed two-stroke diesel engine. Rudolf Diesel patented his engine in 1892 and demonstrated it in 1897. The first marine diesel was installed in the Selandia (Danish, 1912). In the 114 years since, the marine diesel has evolved from a novelty to a thermodynamic masterpiece achieving brake thermal efficiencies exceeding 55%. No other heat engine in production service achieves this.",null],[null,"Connection to earlier volumes","Vol.3: propeller design gives shaft speed n_prop and propulsive efficiency eta_D used in engine selection (Sheet 9). Vol.12: resistance R_T and required power P_D from Holtrop-Mennen used directly in engine matching (Sheet 9). Vol.15: engine room corrosion protection applies to engine components. Vol.16: engine excitation orders and torsional critical speeds connect to Sheet 6. Vol.18: gas turbines and steam turbines already covered. This volume completes the propulsion picture.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Diesel cycle thermodynamics: air standard diesel cycle, dual cycle, IMEP, BMEP, thermal efficiency, SFC from first principles. Two-stroke operation: MAN B&W ME series, WinGD X series, uniflow scavenging, key parameters. Four-stroke operation: Wärtsilä W46F, MAN 32/44, valve timing, Miller cycle. Turbocharging: thermodynamics, charge air cooler, scavenge pressure. Engine performance: propeller law, MCR, NCR, slow steaming, part-load SFC. Fuel systems: HFO, VLSFO, LNG dual-fuel, methanol, ammonia. Emissions: MARPOL Annex VI NOx Tier I/II/III limits, EGR, SCR, EEDI, CII. Engine selection: matching to resistance and propeller. Maintenance: crankshaft deflection, liner wear, condition monitoring.",null],[null,"Primary references","Taylor D.A. (1996) Introduction to Marine Engineering 2nd ed. Butterworth-Heinemann. Woodyard D. (2009) Pounder's Marine Diesel Engines and Gas Turbines 9th ed. Butterworth-Heinemann. Heywood J.B. (1988) Internal Combustion Engine Fundamentals. McGraw-Hill. Watson N. and Janota M.S. (1982) Turbocharging the Internal Combustion Engine. Macmillan. MAN Energy Solutions (2021) Basic Principles of Ship Propulsion. MAN ES. MAN Energy Solutions (2022) Marine Engine Programme. MAN ES. IMO (2016) MARPOL Annex VI and NOx Technical Code 2008.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, connections to earlier volumes, references"],[null,"2","DIESEL CYCLE","Air standard diesel cycle, dual cycle, IMEP, BMEP, SFC"],[null,"3","TWO-STROKE","MAN B&W ME series, WinGD X series, uniflow scavenging"],[null,"4","FOUR-STROKE","Wärtsilä, MAN, valve timing, Miller cycle, vs two-stroke"],[null,"5","TURBOCHARGING","TC thermodynamics, charge air cooler, scavenge pressure"],[null,"6","PERFORMANCE","Propeller law, MCR, NCR, slow steaming, part-load SFC"],[null,"7","FUEL SYSTEMS","HFO, VLSFO, LNG dual-fuel, methanol, ammonia"],[null,"8","EMISSIONS","MARPOL NOx Tier I/II/III, EGR, SCR, SOx, EEDI, CII"],[null,"9","ENGINE SELECTION","Matching engine to resistance and propeller from Vol.12 and Vol.3"],[null,"10","MAINTENANCE","Crankshaft deflection, liner wear, condition monitoring"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","Engine programme data, fuel properties, NOx curves, SFC charts"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
{"kind":"table","sheet":"DIESEL CYCLE","address":"A1:E76","rows":76,"cols":5,"values":[[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"DIESEL CYCLE THERMODYNAMICS -- AIR STANDARD AND DUAL CYCLE",null,null,null],[null,"The air standard diesel cycle is the idealised thermodynamic cycle of the die...",null,null,null],[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"DIESEL AND DUAL CYCLE INPUTS",null,null,null]],"valuesTruncated":true,"valuesPreviewRows":5,"valuesPreviewCols":5,"valuesPreviewAddress":"A1:E5"}
