{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 21 :  Welding Engineering",null,null],[null,"Weld Design, Strength Calculations, Metallurgy and Residual Stresses",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The invisible backbone of every ship","Every ship afloat is held together by welds. A large container ship contains approximately 400 to 600 km of weld seams. Every one of those welds is a potential stress concentration, a potential fatigue crack initiation site, and a potential source of hydrogen induced cracking if the procedure was wrong. The naval architect who designs the structure must understand what happens at the weld. The stress concentrations at the weld toe can reduce the fatigue life of a structural detail by an order of magnitude compared to the parent plate. The HAZ softening in high-strength steel can reduce the local yield strength by 20 to 30%. The residual stresses locked into a weld can equal the yield strength.",null],[null,"The Liberty ship lesson","During World War II approximately 1,500 Liberty ships were built rapidly using all-welded construction for the first time. Several ships broke in two at sea. The SS Schenectady (1943) fractured in calm water in port. The SS John P. Gaines sank within minutes. Root cause: brittle fracture initiated at weld defects in steel with inadequate toughness at low temperature. These disasters drove the development of fracture mechanics, mandatory toughness testing (Charpy V-notch), and the modern classification society welding rules that every ship must comply with today. Source: Masubuchi K. (1980) Analysis of Welded Structures. Ch.1.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Welding processes and heat input: SMAW, FCAW, SAW, GMAW. Heat input formula Q = V x I x 60 / (v x 1000). HAZ metallurgy: Rosenthal (1941) temperature distribution, four HAZ zones, carbon equivalent (CEIIW), preheat temperature (Seferian formula), hydrogen induced cracking mechanism. Weld joint design: butt, fillet, T-joint, ISO 2553 weld symbols, minimum sizes. Weld strength: AWS D1.1 fillet weld, Eurocode EN 1993-1-8, butt weld, combined loading. Residual stresses: why they form, Masubuchi (1980) distribution, angular distortion, longitudinal shrinkage, distortion control. Fatigue at welds: IIW (2008) FAT classes, S-N curves, Palmgren-Miner rule. Fracture mechanics: LEFM, CTOD, Charpy, Liberty ship lesson. Weld defects and inspection: NDT methods, ISO 5817 acceptance criteria. Shipbuilding standards: IACS UR W series, DNV Rules, AWS D1.1.",null],[null,"Primary references","Lancaster J.F. (1999) Metallurgy of Welding 6th ed. Abington Publishing. Maddox S.J. (1991) Fatigue Strength of Welded Structures 2nd ed. Abington Publishing. IIW (2008) Recommendations for Fatigue Design of Welded Joints. Doc IIW-1823-07. Masubuchi K. (1980) Analysis of Welded Structures. Pergamon Press. AWS D1.1 (2020) Structural Welding Code -- Steel. American Welding Society. Rosenthal D. (1941) Mathematical theory of heat distribution during welding. Welding Journal 20(5):220-234. EN 1993-1-8 (2005) Eurocode 3: Design of Steel Structures Part 1-8. Anderson T.L. (2017) Fracture Mechanics 4th ed. CRC Press. DNV Rules for Classification (2022) Part 2 Chapter 3 Welding. IACS (2022) Unified Requirements W series.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, Liberty ship lesson, references"],[null,"2","PROCESSES AND HI","SMAW, FCAW, SAW, GMAW, heat input formula, arc energy"],[null,"3","HAZ METALLURGY","Rosenthal, four HAZ zones, CEIIW, preheat, cold cracking"],[null,"4","JOINT DESIGN","Joint types, fillet geometry, ISO 2553 symbols, minimum sizes"],[null,"5","WELD STRENGTH","AWS fillet, Eurocode EN1993-1-8, butt weld, combined loading"],[null,"6","RESIDUAL STRESSES","Why they form, distribution, distortion, PWHT"],[null,"7","FATIGUE AT WELDS","IIW FAT classes, S-N curves, Palmgren-Miner, hot spot stress"],[null,"8","FRACTURE MECHANICS","LEFM, K_IC, CTOD, Charpy toughness, Liberty ships"],[null,"9","DEFECTS AND NDT","Defect types, VT/PT/MT/UT/RT, ISO 5817 acceptance criteria"],[null,"10","STANDARDS","IACS UR W series, DNV Rules, AWS D1.1, WPS/PQR/WPQ"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","FAT classes, CE values, preheat chart, S-N curves"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
