{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 22 :  Shipbuilding Welding Practice",null,null],[null,"Block Construction, Fit-up, Production Welding, Repair and Automation",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"From the drawing board to the dry dock","Volume 21 explained the engineering science of welding. This volume explains what actually happens in the shipyard. The modern shipyard is a precision manufacturing facility. A large container ship is built as 50 to 150 individual steel blocks, each weighing 100 to 600 tonnes, fabricated in parallel in covered workshops and then transported to the building dock for final assembly. The entire system depends on dimensional accuracy at every stage: if a block is 5mm out of tolerance in one direction, it may not fit against its neighbour, forcing expensive rework on the dock. This volume gives the quantitative tools to manage fit-up, tolerances, production welding, weld repair, and the emerging world of robotic welding.",null],[null,"Connection to Vol.21","Vol.21 covered: HAZ metallurgy, weld strength calculations, residual stresses, fatigue at welds, fracture mechanics, NDT methods, WPS/PQR/WPQ qualification, IACS and DNV standards. This volume assumes knowledge of Vol.21 and builds the practical layer on top. A student who masters both volumes understands welding from the crystal structure of the HAZ to the production rate of a panel line.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Block construction: zone outfitting, grand block assembly, erection sequence. Fit-up and dimensional tolerances: root gap, misalignment, angular mismatch, ISO 13920 general tolerances for welded constructions. Production welding sequences: panel fabrication, one-sided welding, ceramic backing and flux backing, vertical and overhead welding. Productivity calculations: deposition rate, metres per shift, arc time fraction, crew size estimation. Weld repair: excavation methods, minimum repair dimensions, buttering technique, temper bead method for repair without PWHT. Automated and robotic welding: welding robots, panel line automation, laser hybrid welding. Hot work permits and shipyard safety.",null],[null,"Primary references","Lamb T. (ed.) (2003) Ship Design and Construction. SNAME. Ch.22 and Ch.23. AWS D3.5 (2010) Guide for Steel Hull Welding. American Welding Society. IACS (2021) Shipbuilding and Repair Quality Standard Part A and Part B. ISO 13920 (1996) Welding -- General Tolerances for Welded Constructions. ISO 5817 (2014) Fusion-Welded Joints -- Quality Levels for Imperfections. Masubuchi K. (1980) Analysis of Welded Structures. Pergamon Press. Lancaster J.F. (1999) Metallurgy of Welding 6th ed. Abington Publishing. AWS D1.1 (2020) Structural Welding Code -- Steel.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, connection to Vol.21, references"],[null,"2","BLOCK CONSTRUCTION","Zone outfitting, grand blocks, erection sequence"],[null,"3","FIT-UP TOLERANCES","Root gap, misalignment, angular mismatch, ISO 13920"],[null,"4","PRODUCTION WELDING","Panel lines, one-sided welding, positions, backing"],[null,"5","PRODUCTIVITY","Deposition rate, arc time, metres per shift, crew size"],[null,"6","ONE-SIDED WELDING","Ceramic backing, flux backing, SAW one-side"],[null,"7","WELD REPAIR","Excavation, minimum repair dimensions, temper bead"],[null,"8","AUTOMATION","Welding robots, panel line automation, laser hybrid"],[null,"9","HOT WORK SAFETY","Hot work permits, fire watch, gas testing, shipyard safety"],[null,"10","WORKED EXAMPLES","Complete worked examples connecting Vol.4 to Vol.22"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","Fit-up limits, deposition rates, repair dimensions, tolerance table"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
{"kind":"table","sheet":"BLOCK CONSTRUCTION","address":"A1:E32","rows":32,"cols":5,"values":[[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"BLOCK CONSTRUCTION -- HOW MODERN SHIPYARDS BUILD SHIPS",null,null,null],[null,"Modern shipbuilding is based on BLOCK CONSTRUCTION. The ship hull is divided ...",null,null,null],[null,null,null,null,null],[null,"BLOCK CONSTRUCTION STAGES",null,null,null]],"valuesTruncated":true,"valuesPreviewRows":5,"valuesPreviewCols":5,"valuesPreviewAddress":"A1:E5"}
