{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 23 :  Yacht Design",null,null],[null,"Stability, Sail Forces, Hull Form and Planing Resistance",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The magic of yachts","A sailing yacht is the only vessel that uses the wind as its primary power source and yet can sail faster than the wind itself in apparent wind terms on a reach. It heels under the force of the sails until the righting moment of the hull and keel exactly balances the heeling moment of the rig. The keel -- a heavy fin of lead or cast iron hanging below the hull -- is the defining element of a sailing yacht. Without it the yacht capsizes. Understanding yacht design means understanding the interplay between three forces acting simultaneously: the aerodynamic force of the wind on the sails, the hydrodynamic force of the water on the hull and keel, and gravity. When these three forces balance, the yacht sails. This is the most beautiful force balance in all of engineering.",null],[null,"What makes yachts different from ships","Ships are designed to not capsize. Sailing yachts are designed to heel significantly AND recover from a knockdown. Ships have engines. Sailing yachts use the wind -- for free -- forever. Ship hull forms are optimised for minimum resistance at displacement speeds. Sailing yacht hull forms must also perform well when heeled 20 to 30 degrees. Motor yachts at planing speeds have fundamentally different physics: dynamic lift replaces buoyancy as the primary support force. This volume introduces all of these differences quantitatively.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Yacht hull form: LWL, BWL, Cp, Cm, Cwp for yachts, Froude number, hull speed. Yacht stability: GM calculation, GZ curve, AVS, ISO 12217 design categories. Keel design: ballast ratio, righting moment from keel, keel hydrodynamics. Sail forces: apparent wind, aerodynamic lift, driving force vs heeling force, centre of effort, heeling moment and equilibrium heel angle. Leeway and balance: CLR, weather helm, leeway angle, rudder forces. Yacht resistance: Delft series, heel resistance, hull speed. Planing motor yachts: Savitsky (1964) method, trim, planing lift, resistance. Offshore regulations: ISO 12217, ISAF OSR, STIX, capsize screening formula. VPP introduction: force balance concept, polar diagram.",null],[null,"Primary references","Larsson L. and Eliasson R. (2000) Principles of Yacht Design 2nd ed. Adlard Coles. Marchaj C.A. (1988) Aero-Hydrodynamics of Sailing. Adlard Coles. Marchaj C.A. (1979) Sailing Theory and Practice. Dodd Mead. Fossati F. (2009) Aero-Hydrodynamics and the Performance of Sailing Yachts. Adlard Coles. Savitsky D. (1964) Hydrodynamic design of planing hulls. Marine Technology 1(1):71-95. ISO 12217-1 (2013) Small Craft -- Stability and Buoyancy Assessment. Van Oossanen P. (1993) Predicting the speed of sailing yachts. SNAME Trans 101:337-397. Gerritsma J. and Keuning J.A. (1992) Sailing yacht performance. HISWA Symposium. ISAF (2020) Offshore Special Regulations 2020-2021.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, what makes yachts different, references"],[null,"2","YACHT HULL FORM","LWL, BWL, Cp, Cm, Froude number, hull speed, L/D ratio"],[null,"3","YACHT STABILITY","GM, GZ curve, AVS, ISO 12217 categories, STIX"],[null,"4","KEEL DESIGN","Ballast ratio, righting moment, keel hydrodynamics"],[null,"5","SAIL FORCES","Apparent wind, aerodynamic lift, driving vs heeling force"],[null,"6","LEEWAY AND BALANCE","CLR, weather helm, leeway angle, rudder forces"],[null,"7","YACHT RESISTANCE","Delft series, heel resistance, hull speed, frictional"],[null,"8","PLANING MOTOR YACHTS","Savitsky (1964) method, trim, planing lift, resistance"],[null,"9","OFFSHORE REGS","ISO 12217, ISAF OSR, STIX, capsize screening formula"],[null,"10","VPP INTRODUCTION","Force balance concept, simplified VPP, polar diagram"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","Form coefficients, stability limits, sail data, charts"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
