{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 24 :  Advanced Yacht Design",null,null],[null,"Rig Design, Multihull Stability, Structural Loads, Performance and Seamanship",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Completing the yacht design picture","Volume 23 built the foundation: hull form, stability, sail forces, keel design, resistance, and planing hulls. This volume completes the yacht design picture with the topics that turn a competent designer into a complete one: How do you size the rig and calculate mast loads? Why is a catamaran fundamentally different from a monohull in stability? How much structure does the hull need to survive ocean waves? How far can a motor yacht go on one tank? How do you hold a yacht safely at anchor in a 30-knot wind? And how does a racing yacht tactician use polar diagrams to win races? These are the questions that fill real yacht design offices every day.",null],[null,"The two yacht worlds","Sailing yachts and motor yachts share the sea but inhabit different physics. The sailing yacht optimises for minimum resistance under sail -- every kilogram of added weight requires more sail area to push it. The motor yacht optimises for range, comfort, and seakeeping -- fuel weight is the dominant constraint. The multihull occupies a third world: extraordinary speed and space but radically different stability physics. This volume covers all three in the context they deserve.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Rig design and mast loads: sloop vs ketch, shroud geometry, mast compression, forestay tension, boom vang loads. Sail area calculations: mainsail, foretriangle, genoa overlap, spinnaker, IRC measured sail area, SA/displacement ratio. Added resistance in waves: Gerritsma and Keuning (1992) method, comfort ratio, motion sickness index. Multihull stability: catamaran righting moment, capsize mechanics, ISO 12217-2 requirements. Yacht structural loads: ISO 12215-5 design pressure, panel scantlings, keel bolt loads. Motor yacht range and fuel: range equation, fuel tank sizing, endurance calculation. Stabilisers and seakeeping: passive and active stabiliser principles. Anchor and mooring: holding power, chain catenary, scope calculation. Performance polars and VMG: polar diagram reading, VMG calculation, racing tactics application.",null],[null,"Primary references","Larsson L. and Eliasson R. (2000) Principles of Yacht Design 2nd ed. Adlard Coles. Marchaj C.A. (1988) Aero-Hydrodynamics of Sailing. Adlard Coles. ISO 12215-5 (2019) Small Craft -- Hull Construction and Scantlings. ISO 12217-2 (2013) Small Craft -- Stability for Multihull Sailing Craft. Gerritsma J. and Keuning J.A. (1992) Sailing yacht performance. HISWA Symposium. Van Oossanen P. (1993) Predicting the speed of sailing yachts. SNAME Trans 101. Taylor D.A. (1996) Introduction to Marine Engineering 2nd ed.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Introduction, connections to Vol.23, references"],[null,"2","RIG DESIGN","Sloop vs ketch, shroud geometry, mast compression, forestay tension"],[null,"3","SAIL AREAS","Mainsail, foretriangle, genoa overlap, spinnaker, IRC measurement"],[null,"4","WAVES AND COMFORT","Added resistance in waves, comfort ratio, motion sickness index"],[null,"5","MULTIHULL STAB","Catamaran righting moment, capsize, ISO 12217-2"],[null,"6","YACHT STRUCTURE","ISO 12215-5 design pressure, panel scantlings, keel bolt loads"],[null,"7","MOTOR YACHT RANGE","Range equation, fuel tank sizing, endurance at speed"],[null,"8","STABILISERS","Passive and active stabiliser principles, roll reduction"],[null,"9","ANCHOR AND MOORING","Holding power, chain catenary, scope, mooring forces"],[null,"10","POLARS AND VMG","Polar diagram, VMG calculation, racing tactics application"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","Rig data, anchor holding factors, comfort ratios, charts"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
