{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 26 :  Damage Stability and Subdivision",null,null],[null,"SOLAS Subdivision. Probabilistic Method. Stockholm Agreement. Damage Control.",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why damage stability matters -- the disasters that changed maritime law","The Estonia disaster (1994): 852 people died when the bow visor failed and water flooded the vehicle deck. The Herald of Free Enterprise (1987): 193 people died when the bow doors were left open and the ship capsized in minutes. The Concordia (2012): 32 people died after grounding and flooding. Every major revision to SOLAS damage stability requirements was driven by a disaster. The regulations exist because real ships sank and real people died. Understanding damage stability is not an academic exercise. It is a professional and moral obligation for every naval architect. Source: IMO (2005) SOLAS consolidated edition. IMO London.",null],[null,"The two methods -- deterministic and probabilistic","DETERMINISTIC METHOD (pre-2009 for most ships): The ship must survive specific assumed flooding cases. One compartment flooded. Two compartments flooded. The ship must remain afloat with positive stability after each case. Simple and conservative but does not reflect the actual probability of different damage scenarios. PROBABILISTIC METHOD (SOLAS 2009 for new ships): Every possible damage case is assigned a probability of occurrence (p-factor) and a probability of survival (s-factor). The sum of all p x s products gives the Attained Subdivision Index A. A must be greater than the Required Index R. More sophisticated, more realistic, more complex to calculate. Source: IMO MSC.216(82) (2006). SOLAS Chapter II-1.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Lost buoyancy method: permeability, lost waterplane area, rise in KG after flooding. Added weight method: flooding as added weight, new displacement, new GM. SOLAS subdivision: floodable length, permissible length, factor of subdivision F. Probabilistic damage stability: p-factor, s-factor, attained index A, required index R. Stockholm Agreement: additional ro-ro passenger ferry requirements. Damage control: cross-flooding, counter-flooding, damage stability management. Watertight integrity: SOLAS Chapter II-1, watertight doors, margin line. Passenger ship requirements: safe return to port, SOLAS special requirements. Worked example: complete damage stability calculation for a cargo ship.",null],[null,"Primary references","SOLAS 1974 (as amended) Chapter II-1 Subdivision and Damage Stability. IMO London. IMO MSC.216(82) (2006) Amendments to SOLAS Chapter II-1 (probabilistic method). Stockholm Agreement (1996) Specific Stability Requirements for Ro-Ro Passenger Ships. IMO MSC.267(85) (2008) International Code on Intact Stability. Derrett D.J. (2006) Ship Stability for Masters and Mates 6th ed. Butterworth-Heinemann. Ch.14 to Ch.18. Barras B. (2004) Ship Stability for Masters and Mates. Butterworth-Heinemann. Eyres D.J. (2007) Ship Stability for Masters and Mates. Butterworth-Heinemann.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Disasters, two methods, about this volume"],[null,"2","LOST BUOYANCY","Permeability, lost waterplane, rise in KG, new GM"],[null,"3","ADDED WEIGHT","Flooding as added weight, new displacement, new KG"],[null,"4","SOLAS SUBDIV","Floodable length, permissible length, factor F, margin line"],[null,"5","PROBABILISTIC","p-factor, s-factor, attained index A, required index R"],[null,"6","STOCKHOLM AGMT","Ro-ro passenger requirements, residual freeboard, water on deck"],[null,"7","DAMAGE CONTROL","Cross-flooding, counter-flooding, damage management"],[null,"8","WATERTIGHT INTEG","SOLAS Ch.II-1, watertight doors, bulkhead deck, margin line"],[null,"9","WORKED EXAMPLE","Complete damage stability calculation for a cargo ship"],[null,"10","PASSENGER SHIPS","Safe return to port, SOLAS special requirements"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","Permeability values, subdivision factors, s-factor formula"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
