{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 27 :  Ship Manoeuvring and Rudder Design",null,null],[null,"Equations of Motion. Turning Circle. Stopping. Nomoto Model. Rudder Design. IMO Standards.",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"Why manoeuvring matters","A ship that cannot turn cannot avoid a collision. A ship that cannot stop cannot avoid a grounding. The IMO's Standards for Ship Manoeuvrability (MSC.137(76), 2002) were introduced because ships were becoming larger and faster while port approaches were becoming more congested. A fully laden VLCC travelling at 15 knots needs 3 to 4 kilometres to stop and nearly 1 kilometre of turning diameter. These are not abstract numbers -- they determine whether a ship can safely enter a port, avoid a vessel in distress, or respond to an emergency at sea. Every naval architect designing a hull must verify that the ship will meet the IMO manoeuvring standards before the vessel is built. Source: IMO MSC.137(76) (2002) Standards for Ship Manoeuvrability.",null],[null,"The physics of turning","A ship turns because the rudder generates a lateral force. This force creates a yawing moment about the ship's centre of gravity. As the ship begins to turn, the hull itself generates lateral hydrodynamic forces that both resist and assist the turning motion. The ship reaches a steady turning circle when all forces and moments balance. The TACTICAL DIAMETER is the perpendicular distance between the ship's original course and her position when she has turned 180 degrees. IMO requires: Tactical Diameter < 5 x LOA. The ADVANCE is the distance travelled forward when the ship turns 90 degrees. IMO requires: Advance < 4.5 x LOA. These are the two most critical manoeuvring criteria for port entry. Source: IMO MSC.137(76) (2002); Brix (1993).",null],[null,"What this volume covers","Equations of motion: surge, sway, yaw. Hydrodynamic derivatives. Added mass coefficients. Linear manoeuvring equations. Turning circle: tactical diameter, advance, transfer, IMO criteria. Stopping: head reach, track reach, crash stop procedure. Nomoto model: first order K-T model, course stability index, zigzag test prediction. Rudder hydrodynamics: lift and drag, CL, rudder force, torque on stock. Rudder design: area ratio, aspect ratio, rudder types, balance ratio. Propeller-rudder interaction: slipstream velocity, effective inflow speed. IMO manoeuvring standards: all 8 criteria from MSC.137(76). Dynamic positioning: DP classes, thrust allocation, position keeping.",null],[null,"Primary references","IMO MSC.137(76) (2002) Standards for Ship Manoeuvrability. IMO London. IMO MSC/Circ.1053 (2002) Explanatory notes to the standards. Nomoto K. et al. (1957) On the steering qualities of ships. ISP 4(35):354-370. Brix J. (ed.) (1993) Manoeuvring Technical Manual. Seehafen-Verlag Hamburg. Lewis E.V. (ed.) (1989) Principles of Naval Architecture Vol.3. SNAME. Ch.9. Clarke D. et al. (1983) Application of manoeuvring criteria. RINA Trans 125.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","Why manoeuvring matters, physics of turning, references"],[null,"2","EQUATIONS MOTION","Surge, sway, yaw, added mass, hydrodynamic derivatives"],[null,"3","TURNING CIRCLE","Tactical diameter, advance, transfer, IMO criteria"],[null,"4","STOPPING","Head reach, track reach, crash stop, IMO criteria"],[null,"5","NOMOTO MODEL","K-T first order model, course stability, zigzag prediction"],[null,"6","RUDDER HYDRO","Rudder lift, drag, CL, rudder force, torque on stock"],[null,"7","RUDDER DESIGN","Area ratio, aspect ratio, rudder types, balance ratio"],[null,"8","PROP-RUDDER INT","Slipstream velocity, effective inflow, thrust deduction"],[null,"9","IMO STANDARDS","All 8 IMO MSC.137(76) manoeuvring criteria"],[null,"10","DYNAMIC POS","DP classes 1 2 3, thrust allocation, position keeping"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","Hydrodynamic derivatives, rudder data, turning data"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
