{"kind":"table","sheet":"COVER","address":"A1:D31","rows":31,"cols":4,"values":[[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"NAVAL ARCHITECTURE",null,null],[null,"TEACHING TOOLKIT",null,null],[null,"VOLUME 28 :  LNG Cargo Systems",null,null],[null,"LNG Properties. Containment Systems. BOG. Cargo Pumps. Reliquefaction. IGC Code.",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"Alper Alacam  |  Naval Architect and Marine Engineer  |  Turkiye  |  2026",null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"ABOUT THIS VOLUME",null,null],[null,"The most demanding cargo in shipping","Liquefied Natural Gas is natural gas (predominantly methane, CH4) that has been cooled to minus 163 degrees Celsius at atmospheric pressure until it liquefies. At this temperature its volume is reduced by a factor of approximately 600 compared with the gas at ambient conditions. This makes it economically viable to transport by sea in large quantities. LNG is the most technically demanding cargo carried by merchant ships. The cryogenic temperatures require specialised insulated tank systems. Any heat ingress causes the cargo to boil, generating gas (Boil-Off Gas or BOG). This BOG must be managed: used as fuel, reliquefied, or vented safely. The entire system -- tanks, pumps, vapour lines, instrumentation, and emergency systems -- operates at conditions that would destroy ordinary materials and equipment. Source: SIGTTO (2016) Liquefied Gas Handling Principles 4th ed.",null],[null,"Why LNG matters now more than ever","LNG is the fastest-growing sector in global shipping. The world's LNG trade grew from 100 million tonnes per year in 2000 to over 400 million tonnes per year by 2023. New markets: Japan, South Korea, China, India, and Europe all import significant LNG volumes. LNG as marine fuel: MARPOL Annex VI sulphur limits and the IMO 2050 decarbonisation targets are driving shipowners to adopt LNG as fuel for container ships, cruise ships, and ferry services. The IGF Code (2015) governs LNG as marine fuel. A naval architect who does not understand LNG systems is not fully equipped for modern ship design. Source: IGF Code (2015); MARPOL Annex VI.",null],[null,"What this volume covers","LNG properties: methane phase diagram, liquefaction at minus 163 deg C, density 430 to 470 kg/m3, LHV = 50,050 kJ/kg, Wobbe index. Cargo containment systems: Moss Rosenberg spherical, GTT Mark III membrane, GTT NO96 membrane, SPB prismatic independent tanks. Boil-off gas management: natural BOG rate calculation, BOG as propulsion fuel, forced vaporisation for gas burning engines. Cargo pump calculations: centrifugal submerged pump, head, flow rate, NPSH. Reliquefaction: nitrogen cycle, Brayton refrigeration, energy consumption. Cargo heating and cooling: heat ingress Q = U x A x delta T, pre-cooling before loading, warming before dry-dock. LNG as marine fuel: Type C tanks, bunkering, IGF Code. IGC Code and safety systems: ESD, pressure relief, inert gas, gas detection.",null],[null,"Primary references","IGC Code (2016) International Code for the Construction and Equipment of Ships Carrying Liquefied Gases in Bulk. IMO London. IGF Code (2015) International Code of Safety for Ships Using Gases or Other Low-Flashpoint Fuels. IMO London. SIGTTO (2016) Liquefied Gas Handling Principles on Ships and in Terminals 4th ed. Society of International Gas Tanker and Terminal Operators. Mokhatab S. et al. (2014) Handbook of Liquefied Natural Gas. Gulf Professional Publishing. MAN Energy Solutions (2022) LNG as Ship Fuel. Taylor D.A. (1996) Introduction to Marine Engineering 2nd ed.",null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"WORKSHEET INDEX",null,null],[null,"No.","Sheet","Contents"],[null,"1","COVER","LNG overview, why it matters, references"],[null,"2","LNG PROPERTIES","Methane phase diagram, density, LHV, Wobbe index"],[null,"3","CONTAINMENT SYS","Moss, GTT Mark III, NO96, SPB comparison"],[null,"4","BOIL-OFF GAS","Natural BOG rate, BOG as fuel, forced vaporisation"],[null,"5","CARGO PUMPS","Submerged pump head, flow rate, NPSH, power"],[null,"6","RELIQUEFACTION","Nitrogen cycle, Brayton refrigeration, energy consumption"],[null,"7","HEAT MANAGEMENT","Heat ingress Q=UAdT, pre-cooling, warm-up"],[null,"8","LNG AS FUEL","Type C tanks, bunkering, IGF Code requirements"],[null,"9","IGC CODE SAFETY","ESD, pressure relief, inert gas, gas detection"],[null,"10","CARGO OPERATIONS","Loading, discharging, inerting, purging sequences"],[null,"11","REFERENCE TABLES","LNG properties, BOG rates, containment comparison"],[null,null,null,null],[null,null,null,null],[null,"© 2026 Alper Alacam Naval Architecture . All rights reserved.",null,null]]}
